@meta
id: 25-02
title: Backwards from the end
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: range-syntax
par: 17
minutes: 3
vscode: scroll to the bottom, select the last three lines, Delete
open: RUNBOOK.md

@brief
The runbook ends with a contact block that points at a rota nobody has
used since the on-call move. The last three lines of the file go — the
heading, the blank line and the sentence. Everything above stays.

You know `$` as the last line. It takes arithmetic like `.` does, so
`$-2` is the third line from the bottom, and you can say "the last three
lines" without knowing how long the file is. That matters more than it
sounds: the same command then works on the file after it has grown.

The cursor can stay on line 1.

@keys
| `:$-2,$d` | the last three lines |
| `$-1` | the second to last line |
| `:$d` | delete the last line |

@hints
Count the lines that must go, from the bottom: three. So the range runs
from two above the end, to the end.
--
Left address `$-2`, right address `$`, command `d`.
--
`:$-2,$d` Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :$-2,$d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twelve keystrokes and no scrolling. The point is that you never looked
at the bottom of the file. You did not need to know whether the runbook
was forty lines or four hundred.

Both ends of a range are just addresses, and every address takes an
offset, so all of these are legal and mean what they look like:

    :$-9,$d       the last ten lines
    :.-2,.+2y     five lines centred on the cursor
    :1,$-1>       everything except the last line
    :$-4,$m0      move the last five lines to the top

One caution: the offsets are evaluated before the command runs, against
the file as it is now. `:$-2,$d` run twice takes six lines, not the same
three. Ranges are not idempotent, and `u` is the fix.

@start RUNBOOK.md
# Payments runbook

## Symptoms

Refunds queue up and never settle. The `refund_queue` depth climbs
steadily and the gateway dashboard shows timeouts.

## First checks

1. `SELECT count(*) FROM refund_queue WHERE settled_at IS NULL;`
2. Gateway status page.
3. The last deploy — `git log --oneline -5 origin/main`.

## Recovery

Drain the queue with `npm run payments:drain`. It is idempotent and
safe to run twice. Watch the depth fall before you walk away.

## Contact

Page the on-call engineer through the old PagerDuty rota.

@expected RUNBOOK.md
# Payments runbook

## Symptoms

Refunds queue up and never settle. The `refund_queue` depth climbs
steadily and the gateway dashboard shows timeouts.

## First checks

1. `SELECT count(*) FROM refund_queue WHERE settled_at IS NULL;`
2. Gateway status page.
3. The last deploy — `git log --oneline -5 origin/main`.

## Recovery

Drain the queue with `npm run payments:drain`. It is idempotent and
safe to run twice. Watch the depth fall before you walk away.
